[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The campus network and the office switch segment serving Laptop2 and Laptop3 are healthy. Both laptops are on the same subnet and L2 segment and have working bidirectional connectivity: Laptop2 (172.16.10.12/24) can ping Laptop3 (172.16.10.13/24) with 0% loss (~1.4 ms), and Laptop3 can ping Laptop2 with 0% loss (~1.1 ms). Their NICs are UP and correctly configured with a /24 mask and default gateway. This rules out switch-port faults, VLAN misconfiguration, or physical issues.

What’s broken is the endpoint file-sharing layer (not the network). On both laptops, no file-sharing or remote-access daemons are listening on any interface: netstat shows only loopback listeners and no SMB/CIFS (139/445), NFS, or SSH (22) services bound to 0.0.0.0. Without a listening service and related discovery (e.g., NetBIOS/mDNS), “seeing each other” in file browsers and accessing shared folders will fail, even though ping works.

Localization: application/service layer on Laptop2 and Laptop3 (not switch1). Root cause: file-sharing services (e.g., smbd/nmbd or an SSH/SFTP server) are not running/listening on either laptop.

Corrective fix: enable a sharing service on at least one laptop. For SMB: install/enable Samba (smbd, nmbd), create a share, and allow it through any host firewall. Alternatively, enable sshd for SFTP/rsync. After starting the service(s), the peers will appear in discovery and file access will work again. No network changes required. 
=== END ===